React is not the bottleneck by default; unbounded re-rendering is. You can beat Obsidian if you treat React as a thin view layer and move real work off the main thread.

**Where React is sufficient**
- UI glue, layout, and small components are fine in React; keep components pure and memoized, use event delegation, and virtualize any list/tree.
- Treat the editor as an island (CodeMirror) and avoid pushing whole-document state through React; send patches/diffs.
- Keep global state small (pane tree, doc metadata) in a store (Zustand/Jotai) and read slices via selectors to prevent cascading renders.

**Where Rust/Native earns its keep**
- File I/O: bulk directory scans, debounced writes, and fs watching are far faster and more predictable in Rust than Node.
- Indexing/search/graph: build the inverted index and link graph in Rust, stream results to the UI (binary or compact JSON). This removes GC pressure and main-thread stalls.
- Heavy parsing (front-matter, math, code fences) and compression/snapshotting belong in Rust or workers, not React.
- Security/plugin sandboxing: native side can gate filesystem/network access for plugins.

**Performance playbook to beat Obsidian**
- Render: virtualize file tree/search lists; avoid layout thrash (no nested flex recalcs on every keystroke). Prefer CSS containment where possible.
- State flow: stable props, `useSyncExternalStore`/selector-based subscription; batch updates. Don’t store derived data in state.
- Work distribution: all expensive work in Web Workers or Rust (indexing, search, previews, linting). Main thread only paints.
- Data movement: send deltas, not blobs. Use shared-array buffers or chunked streams for large results.
- Startup: lazy-load panes/plugins; ship a minimal shell bundle, precompile CodeMirror extensions.
- Profiling: set budgets (e.g., TTI < 800 ms, input latency < 16 ms, search < 150 ms on 5k notes) and measure with React Profiler + flamegraphs on Rust side.

**Is an all-React shell enough?**
- Yes, if you keep React out of hot loops and let Rust/workers handle IO and indexing. The combination justifies the Rust backend because it removes main-thread load and lets you scale vault size without jank.

**If you want “super-performative”**
- Keep React minimal; use fine-grained subscriptions, virtualize everything large, push heavy logic to Rust/workers, and enforce perf budgets with profiling. That’s the path to matching or beating Obsidian’s feel.

